  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 343 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\n\nRest of Monday and Tonight... \n\nA few isolated showers and thunderstorms (primarily near and \nnorth of the KS / NE state line) cannot be ruled out this \nafternoon into evening, however a substantial cap casts a good \ndeal of doubt on these storms successfully initiating. \nRegardless, if storms develop, no severe weather is expected. \n\nAs for tonight, a glancing blow of showers and a few \nthunderstorms is expected for our far northwestern CWA as a \njoint mid-level and low-level jet impinge on the area through \nWednesday morning. No flooding is expected from these showers. \n\nTuesday...\n\nThe main show of the week arrives on Tuesday. Our initial jet \nstreak will gradually pull off to the northeast through Tuesday \nmorning, but a secondary pulse will arrive round noon. Vorticity\nadvection is not expected to ever fully die down, and thus \nbroken rounds of showers and storms are expected through the \nday. The likeliest times for precip will be from noon onwards, \nwith the likeliest time for a break in the mid-morning between \njet streaks. \n\nTuesday night (particularly 1 AM - 4 AM Wednesday morning) is \nexpected to strongly uptick the intensity of precip (and thus \nflooding risk). At this time, the remnants of Hurricane Polo \nwill advance into our area after getting sucked into our main \ntrough. Vorticity advection, a favorable mid-level jet with \nstrong diffluence, a 35+ kt low-level jet, and warm air \nadvection all look to arrive with saturated profiles through the\ndepth of the troposphere. By sunrise on Wednesday morning, mean\n24-hr precip of 0.5 - 1.5 inches (with the heaviest favored \nsouth of I-80) is expected. As a potential worst-case scenario, \n12z HREF 95th- percentile 24-hr totals by sunup on Wednesday \nexceed 3.5 inches with the heaviest yet to come on WED. Given \nantecedent wet soils, flooding will be a concern. WPC currently \nholds us in a Level 2 out of 4 Slight Risk for flash flood \nexceedance for TUE. \n\nWednesday...\n\nWednesday is highly likely to be a washout with rain and storms\ncarrying us through the day. Our trough, jets, and saturated \nprofiles with PWATs >150% of normal for this time of year will \ncontinue to provide for additional flooding risk. WPC maintains \nus us in a Level 2 out of 4 Slight Risk for flash flood \nexceedance on WED. 13z NBM guidance suggests new Wednesday and \nWednesday night rainfall totals of 1.5 - 3 inches, with 95th-\npercentile wosrt-case totals locally exceeding 5 inches in our \ncentral CWA. Of note for our southern CWA, where forcing remains \nmore optimal, a few strong to severe thunderstorms will be \npossible, with SPC currently outlining these counties in a Level 1\nout of 5 Marginal Risk for severe thunderstorms.\n\nExactly where the heaviest rain falls will depend on how far \nnorthward the nose of the jets can reach, and the northern edge \nof the jet will likely produce a sharp cutoff in rainfall \ntotals. THE EPS and GEFS tend to favor our central CWA as the \nnorthern edge, while the 12z REFS favors our northern counties. \nIn addition, some breaks in rainfall are expected, but dry time \nwill be the exception and not the rule. \n\n\nThursday...\n\nPrecip looks to linger on Thursday, primarily for our KS \ncounties. By evening, precip is expected to finally die off as \nour favorable forcing ejects to the east. Despite the heavy \nrainfall coming to an end, the trough core itself will likely \nlinger on Thursday, bringing cooler temperature in the low 60s F \nwith overnight lows into FRI able to dip into the mid 40s. \nAdditionally, breezier conditions with wind gusts of 25 - 30 mph \nare expected. \n\nIn all, by sundown on Thursday when rain is expected to end, \nlikeliest cumulative totals across the CWA look to be 2.5 - 4 \ninches, with a worst-case scenario of 5+ inches per 13z NBM \nguidance. \n\nFriday into Weekend... \n\nFriday, Saturday, and Sunday are expected to stay dry with \nrelatively cooler temperatures prevailing. The 06s GEFS suggests\nthat the next shortwave trough will move in from Canada on \nSaturday, but right now precipitation associated with this \ntrough looks unlikely.",
